incident 4924ff36-f242-48bc-b686-4e293ece9386  state triaging  anchor 21:00:16
triage: 7 services, severity warning, start from ad, 8 unmeasured edge(s) crossed

states: triaging -> planning -> investigating -> synthesizing -> proposing
trajectory: 6ff4f817-f1dd-494b-a686-80c419102492
triage judged: investigate (low confidence, suspects feature_flag)
  Two independent services alerted simultaneously, which is more than a single quiet latency warning and not dismissible. flagd appears as a shared candidate cause for ad, making a flag change a reasonable cheap prior.
investigation: 2 round(s), 8 dispatch(es), 24 step(s)

=== PROPOSAL ===
  action      : restart_service (restart)
  target      : ad
  expect      : Recreating ad's container should end the log silence that has held since 20:59:11. Within roughly one JVM cold start (the previous one took ~17s from first line to 'listening on gRPC port' [tr_ce520f9e7393]) Loki on {namespace="astronomy-shop",pod=~"ad-..."} should show a fresh startup sequence followed by ordinary per-request ad lines carrying populated trace ids, and no further 'gRPC shutdown / JVM shutdown' line. Note the confirmation must be log-based: ad emits no trace-derived span metrics, so error-ratio and latency percentiles do not exist for it in Prometheus [tr_1c44b72de6bd], and Tempo held no ad spans at all [tr_2178f15d99cb] — if spans do reappear in Tempo after the restart that is a bonus signal, not a required one.
  within      : 300s
  risk        : restart_service is not reversible. If the 20:59:11 stop was commanded by something still in force — an operator-driven teardown, a scheduler eviction, a supervisor — the recreate either fails or is undone, and we have spent the one irreversible action while learning less than a pod-state/exit-code query would have told us for free. If ad was in fact already restarted by the platform after 20:59:11 (unverified), this recreate interrupts a working process and causes a second, self-inflicted outage window. It also discards in-process state; for ad that is cache only, so the cost is a cold cache and slower first responses, not data. And it closes off post-mortem inspection of the stopped container's exit code.
  blast radius: One service for the duration of the recreate. This brief names only ad's outbound edge (ad → flagd), not its callers, so the inbound set is not fully enumerated here — operators should assume the services that render ads see connection-refused or reset during the recreate, and should check the frontend path specifically. flagd sees ad's flag-evaluation traffic resume, which it logged no errors handling [tr_f75ff8f53156]. product-catalog, checkout, recommendation and frontend-proxy are untouched by this action; if their alerts clear, that is evidence of a causal link that has not otherwise been established.
  if wrong    : Three falsifiers. (1) No startup lines appear within five minutes — the container is not being scheduled, and the mechanism is at the orchestrator, not the process. (2) Startup lines appear and are followed within a minute or two by another JVM-driven graceful shutdown — something external is signalling ad to stop (the open question of what sent the 20:59:11 signal was never answered; no pod state, restart count, or exit code was retrieved), and repeating the restart will just churn. (3) ad logs resume and serve normally but product-catalog is still alerting — the causal link from ad to product-catalog was never shown (its unfiltered logs were never queried [tr_181fc5b26702], its span metrics do not exist [tr_f2b13604310b]), so a shared upstream trigger would remain live and this restart would have fixed a symptom.
  rests on    : tr_ce520f9e7393, tr_2178f15d99cb, tr_03b9ea884053, tr_1c44b72de6bd
  execution   : not executed - a proposal is a claim, never the change (ADR-0028 §1)

context: 31994 pushed / 2914 pulled (pull rate 0.0835), 2 section(s) dropped

=== VERDICT ===
  fault class : process_freeze
  fix class   : restart
  confidence  : medium
  evidence    : tr_ce520f9e7393, tr_2178f15d99cb, tr_03b9ea884053, tr_f75ff8f53156, tr_df44850fa5b2, tr_1c44b72de6bd, tr_f2b13604310b, tr_181fc5b26702
  root cause  : The ad service stopped serving at 20:59:11 and never came back. Its own logs show a cold start at 20:58:05, four normal ad requests through 20:59:10 with intact trace context and no errors, then a JVM-driven gRPC shutdown at 20:59:11 followed by total silence across the 21:00:16 alert [tr_ce520f9e7393]. Tempo holds no ad spans at all for the window [tr_2178f15d99cb]. Ad's own output never complains about an unreachable peer, a full disk, a wrong address or credential, or memory pressure — it simply goes quiet after a signalled stop, which is the silent-culprit signature. Ad's only change records are first-creation entries by platform-automation, not a rollout, config edit, or limit change [tr_03b9ea884053], and flagd came up cleanly, parsed its flag file, and logged no reload or evaluation errors [tr_f75ff8f53156, tr_df44850fa5b2]. The mechanism now is a stopped ad process behind a still-advertised service endpoint; the fix is to bring ad back up.
  OPEN        : What signalled the 20:59:11 shutdown, and did the container restart afterwards? No pod state, restart count, or exit code was retrieved, which is why this is medium confidence.
  OPEN        : Do ad's callers hang to a deadline or fail fast with connection-refused? No caller-side traces or socket evidence was collected, and that distinction separates a frozen-but-listening process from a dead one.
  OPEN        : Why did product-catalog alert at the same second? Its unfiltered logs were never queried and its span metrics do not exist, so no causal link from ad has been shown and a shared upstream trigger is untested.

